%% CASE Seabreeze island resort expansion
Seabreeze Resorts is adding 120 villas to its island resort and building a new guest app for bookings and services. Construction follows a predictive plan; an agile team builds the app.

- The resort's owners (a family trust) and an international hotel operator share governance. Any decision above **$500,000** needs the approval of both parties.
- The island's coastal protection law bans construction within **50 metres of the shoreline** during the turtle nesting season (May to August).
- The **hurricane season** (June to November) can stop work for days at a time, and materials arrive from the mainland by ship only every two weeks.
- Front-desk staff fear that the new app, which offers online check-in, will **replace their jobs**.
@@ BE1 | Hybrid
The operator's project director wants to approve a $700,000 design change alone, to save time. What should the project manager do?
A) Follow the agreed governance, which requires approval from both the owners and the operator
B) Let the operator approve it, since the operator will run the resort once it has been built
C) Split the change into two smaller changes, so that each one stays below the $500,000 limit
D) Ask the construction contractor to decide, since the contractor will carry out the design change
= A
? Governance defines decision rights. A decision above the threshold needs both parties' approval; splitting the change to avoid the threshold would be unethical.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
How should the construction plan address the coastal protection law?
A) Schedule shoreline work outside the nesting season and treat the ban as a schedule constraint
B) Continue shoreline work during the nesting season and pay any fines as part of the project's costs
C) Carry out the shoreline work at night during the nesting season, when inspectors are not present
D) Ask the owners to lobby the government for an exemption and continue the work in the meantime
= A
? Legal requirements are built into the plan as constraints. Ignoring the law, paying fines as a cost of doing business or hiding work are compliance and ethical breaches.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
Which response best addresses the hurricane threat to the construction schedule?
A) Mitigate it with secure storage, buffer stock and contingency, triggered by weather warnings
B) Accept it, since hurricanes are force majeure events and the contract will excuse any delays
C) Transfer it to the shipping company by requiring it to guarantee deliveries every two weeks
D) Ignore it until a hurricane warning is issued, since storms are impossible to predict in advance
= A
? Mitigation reduces both the probability of delays and their impact. Force majeure may excuse contractual obligations, but it does not remove the schedule impact on the project.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
How should the project address the front-desk staff's fears about the new app?
A) Explain how roles will change, involve staff in the workflows and train them for new tasks
B) Tell the staff that their jobs will not change at all, so that they stop worrying about the app
C) Say nothing about the app until launch day, so that the staff have less time to worry about it
D) Leave the matter to human resources, since the project only delivers the app and the villas
= A
? Honest communication, involvement and training reduce resistance. False assurances or secrecy damage trust when the truth emerges.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Predictive
Two projects in the same programme have conflicting objectives: one needs a system frozen for six months, while the other needs to change it. Who should resolve the conflict?
A) The programme manager, through programme governance
B) The two project managers, by negotiating between themselves
C) The project team members who use the system most
D) The vendor that supplies the system to both projects
= A
? Conflicts between projects in a programme are resolved at programme level, where the trade-offs can be made against the programme's benefits.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
Stakeholders keep reopening decisions that were made months ago, and nobody can remember why the decisions were taken. What would help most?
A) A decision log that records each decision, its rationale and who approved it
B) Longer steering committee meetings, so that every decision can be discussed again in full
C) A rule that no decision may ever be revisited once the steering committee has made it
D) Asking the project manager to remember the key decisions and explain them when asked
= A
? A decision log provides transparency and an audit trail, which reduces repeated debates. Decisions can still be revisited, but deliberately and with full information.

@@ BE1 | Predictive | multi
A steering committee member owns shares in one of the companies bidding for a major contract. What should the member do? (Choose two.)
A) Declare the conflict of interest
B) Abstain from the related decision
C) Vote for the company to show confidence
D) Keep the shareholding private
E) Ask the bidder to withdraw its proposal
= A, B
? A conflict of interest must be disclosed, and the person concerned should not take part in the related decision. This protects the fairness of the process.

@@ BE1 | Agile
The IT change advisory board meets monthly and must approve every production release, so the agile teams can release only once a month. What should the project manager propose?
A) A pre-approved route for low-risk releases with automated tests and controls
B) Releasing without the board's approval, since agile teams should be autonomous
C) Combining all features into one large release each quarter to reduce the board's workload
D) Asking the board to meet daily so that it can approve each release as soon as it is ready
= A
? Governance can be adapted to agile delivery, for example by pre-approving standard, low-risk changes that meet agreed controls, while higher-risk changes still go to the board.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
A small, low-risk internal project must follow the same governance as the organisation's largest projects, including monthly board reviews. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree tailored governance with the PMO that fits the project's size and risk
B) Skip the board reviews, since the project is too small to need governance
C) Follow the full process, since the same governance must apply to every project
D) Ask the sponsor to cancel the project, since the governance costs too much
= A
? Governance should be tailored to the project's size, complexity and risk. Tailoring is agreed with the governing bodies rather than decided unilaterally.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
To speed up a building permit, a local official asks the project manager for an unofficial "facilitation payment". What should the project manager do?
A) Refuse and report the request in line with anti-corruption law and the organisation's policy
B) Pay it, since small payments of this kind are customary in the region and speed up the work
C) Pay it through the contractor, so that the organisation is not directly involved in the payment
D) Refuse, but say nothing to anyone, in order to avoid damaging the relationship with the official
= A
? Facilitation payments are bribes under many anti-corruption laws and violate the PMI Code of Ethics. The request is refused and reported according to policy.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
A research study will collect data from human participants. What must happen before any data is collected?
A) Approval from the research ethics committee
B) Publication of the study's expected results
C) A press release announcing the study
D) Agreement from the funder's marketing team
= A
? Research involving human participants requires ethics approval before data collection. The time needed for approval is built into the plan.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
The e-commerce analytics team wants to keep all customer data indefinitely, but data protection law requires data to be kept only as long as necessary. What should the project do?
A) Apply retention rules, keeping only the data needed and deleting or anonymising the rest
B) Keep all of the data, since it might become useful for future analyses and machine learning
C) Delete all customer data immediately to remove any compliance risk
D) Move the data to another country where retention rules do not apply
= A
? Data retention rules are compliance requirements. The design keeps what is necessary for a lawful purpose and deletes or anonymises the rest.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
To meet the schedule, the plan requires installation crews to work 70-hour weeks, which exceeds the legal limit on working time. What should the project manager do?
A) Re-plan within the legal limits, for example by adding crews or extending the schedule
B) Keep the plan, since the crews have agreed in writing to work the extra hours voluntarily
C) Keep the plan, but record only the legal maximum hours on the crews' timesheets
D) Ask the crews to work as independent contractors so that the limits no longer apply
= A
? Plans must comply with labour law. Falsifying records or disguising employment to avoid the law is illegal and unethical.

@@ BE2 | Agile
Marketing wants the online shop to show prices without mandatory fees so that products look cheaper. Consumer law requires the total price to be shown. What should the product owner do?
A) Explain the legal requirement and adjust the design so that total prices are shown
B) Show the lower prices, since the fees are displayed at the final checkout step anyway
C) Let marketing decide, since pricing is a marketing responsibility, not a product one
D) Show the lower prices until a regulator or a customer complains about the practice
= A
? Legal requirements are non-negotiable constraints on the product. The product owner ensures that the design complies and explains why to stakeholders.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
Where should all change requests and their status be recorded?
A) In the change log
B) In the lessons learned register
C) In the project charter
D) In the stakeholder register
= A
? The change log records all change requests and their status, such as approved, rejected or pending.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
The team retrains its AI recommendation model every month with new data, and the model's behaviour changes each time. How should these changes be controlled?
A) Treat model versions as configuration items that are tested and approved before release
B) Release each retrained model automatically, since the training process is the same each time
C) Stop retraining the model, since any change in its behaviour creates risks for the business
D) Let the data scientists decide informally when a new model version is good enough
= A
? AI models change as their data changes. Version control, testing against agreed criteria and approval before release keep these changes controlled and traceable.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
A change requested in the agile part of a project requires a change to hardware that is being built under a baselined, predictive plan. How should the change be handled?
A) Through formal change control for the hardware, coordinated with the product owner
B) By the product owner alone, since the request came from the agile part of the project
C) By the hardware team alone, since the hardware plan has its own baselines
D) By implementing it informally, since hybrid projects do not need change control
= A
? In hybrid projects, a change that affects baselined work goes through formal change control, while backlog impacts are coordinated with the product owner.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
The project is behind schedule. To make the variance disappear, a team member suggests updating the schedule baseline to match the actual progress. What should the project manager do?
A) Refuse, since baselines change only through approved change requests
B) Agree, since the baseline should always reflect the latest progress
C) Agree, but keep the old baseline as a hidden copy for reference
D) Ask the team to report the original dates while updating the baseline
= A
? Baselines are the reference for measuring performance. Changing them to hide variances is not allowed; they change only through approved change requests.

@@ BE3 | Agile
A market change makes the current sprint goal obsolete in the middle of the sprint. Who can cancel the sprint?
A) The product owner
B) The scrum master
C) The developers, by majority vote
D) The sponsor's assistant
= A
? In Scrum, only the product owner has the authority to cancel a sprint, which happens when the sprint goal becomes obsolete.

@@ BE3 | Predictive | match
Match each type of change request with its purpose.
1) Corrective action
2) Preventive action
3) Defect repair
4) Update
A) Bring future performance back in line with the plan
B) Reduce the probability of negative performance in the future
C) Modify a nonconforming product or deliverable
D) Change a formally controlled document or plan
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? These are the common types of change requests processed through change control.

@@ BE4 | Agile
The product owner works on the project only part-time, and the team often waits days for decisions. What should the scrum master do?
A) Raise it with the sponsor to secure more availability or a delegate with decision authority
B) Let the developers make the product decisions themselves while the product owner is away
C) Accept the delays, since the product owner also has other important responsibilities
D) Ask the team to start the next items without clarification and fix any problems later
= A
? An unavailable product owner is an impediment. The scrum master escalates it so that the team gets timely decisions from someone with the right authority.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
An unforeseen problem occurs that was not in the risk register, and the team improvises a response. What is this response called?
A) A workaround
B) A contingency plan
C) A fallback plan
D) A risk trigger
= A
? A workaround is an unplanned response to an issue. Contingency and fallback plans are prepared in advance for identified risks.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
A key laboratory instrument breaks down, halting experiments, and the repair will take six weeks. What should the project manager do?
A) Look for alternatives, such as partner labs or resequencing, and escalate if needed
B) Pause all of the research work until the instrument has been repaired and fully tested again
C) Ask the researchers to repair the instrument themselves to save waiting time
D) Remove the affected experiments from the research plan to stay on schedule
= A
? Issues are addressed by analysing options to reduce their impact and escalating when decisions are needed beyond the project manager's authority.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
Several issues in the issue log have "the team" as their owner, and none of them is making progress. What should the project manager do?
A) Assign a single named owner and a due date to each issue
B) Discuss all of the issues at every weekly team meeting
C) Close the issues that have been open for more than a month
D) Escalate all of the issues to the sponsor at once
= A
? Clear individual ownership and due dates create accountability, so that issues are actively driven to resolution.

@@ BE4 | Agile
On the team's Kanban board, several cards have been marked as blocked for days, while team members keep starting new work. What should the team do?
A) Focus on unblocking those items before starting new work
B) Move the blocked cards back to the backlog to clear the board
C) Raise the WIP limits so that more new work can be started
D) Wait for the blocking teams to contact them when ready
= A
? "Stop starting, start finishing": swarming on blocked items improves flow, while starting new work only increases work in progress.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
A server failure that nobody had anticipated stopped testing for two days. The problem has now been solved. What should the project manager do next?
A) Update the risk register for similar risks and record the lesson learned
B) Close the issue and move on, since the problem has already been solved
C) Find the team member responsible for the server and report the failure
D) Add two days of buffer to every remaining activity in the schedule
= A
? Unforeseen issues often reveal gaps in risk identification. Updating the risk register and capturing the lesson improves future risk management.

@@ BE5 | Predictive | fill
A threat has a 40% probability and an impact of $200,000. A response costing $30,000 would reduce its probability to 10%. What is the net benefit of the response? (Answer in dollars.)
= $30,000
? EMV before = 0.40 × 200,000 = $80,000. EMV after = 0.10 × 200,000 = $20,000. Reduction = $60,000. Net benefit = 60,000 − 30,000 = $30,000.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
The project manager wants to make sure that risk identification covers all of the main sources of risk. Which tool helps most?
A) A risk breakdown structure used as a checklist of categories
B) A probability and impact matrix used to rank the risks
C) A list of risks that the sponsor considers to be important
D) The risk register from the last project, used without changes
= A
? A risk breakdown structure organises risk sources into categories, which helps make sure that no major area is overlooked during identification.

@@ BE5 | Agile
Before the project starts, the team imagines that the project has failed and lists all of the reasons why. What is this technique called?
A) A pre-mortem
B) A retrospective
C) A post-mortem
D) A sprint review
= A
? A pre-mortem uses imagined failure to surface risks early, while there is still time to plan responses.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
Two companies form a partnership to bid for a large contract that neither could win on its own. Which opportunity response is this?
A) Share
B) Exploit
C) Enhance
D) Accept
= A
? Sharing an opportunity allocates ownership to a third party that is best able to capture the benefit, for example through a partnership or joint venture.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
How can a project prepare for risks that cannot be identified in advance?
A) Build resilience through reserves, flexible processes and short feedback loops
B) Add every possible risk to the risk register, however unlikely it seems
C) Accept that nothing can be done until an unidentified risk actually occurs
D) Buy insurance against all of the risks that the project might ever face during its life
= A
? Emergent risks (unknown unknowns) are handled through resilience: management reserves, adaptable processes and rapid detection and response.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid | multi
Which two are examples of transferring a risk? (Choose two.)
A) Buying insurance
B) Using a fixed-price contract to shift cost risk
C) Adding a buffer to the schedule
D) Building a prototype first
E) Monitoring the risk monthly
= A, B
? Transfer shifts the impact and ownership of a threat to a third party, for example an insurer or a seller. Buffers and prototypes are mitigation or contingency measures.

@@ BE6 | Agile
The team believes that limiting work in progress will reduce its cycle time. How should it introduce the change?
A) As a timeboxed experiment with a measurable hypothesis, reviewed afterwards
B) As a permanent rule, since WIP limits are known to work for every team
C) Only after the organisation has approved a new process standard for all teams
D) Without measuring anything, since the team will feel whether it works or not
= A
? Continuous improvement uses small experiments with clear hypotheses and measures, so that the team learns whether a change really helps.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
Approved documents wait in an inbox for five days before anyone starts the next step. Which type of lean waste is this?
A) Waiting
B) Overproduction
C) Motion
D) Overprocessing
= A
? Waiting is time when work is idle between steps. Reducing it shortens lead time without anyone working harder.

@@ BE6 | Agile
After a production outage, the team wants to learn from what happened without people becoming defensive. What should it hold?
A) A blameless post-incident review focused on systems and processes
B) A meeting to identify the person who caused the outage
C) A management review that decides on disciplinary action
D) A short meeting in which the outage is not mentioned again
= A
? Blameless reviews encourage honest reporting and focus on improving systems and processes, which prevents recurrence more effectively than blame.

@@ BE6 | Hybrid
Some installation crews have found faster and safer ways of fitting the meters. How should these improvements be spread across the project?
A) Standardise the improved method and train all crews in it
B) Let each crew decide whether it wants to use the new method
C) Ask the best crews to do all of the remaining installations
D) Record the improvement in the final report for future projects
= A
? Standard work captures improvements so that everyone benefits, and it becomes the baseline for further improvement.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
Throughput is limited by a single inspection station. According to the theory of constraints, what should be done first?
A) Make full use of the constraint before adding capacity
B) Buy a second inspection station straight away
C) Speed up all of the other stations in the process
D) Increase the amount of work sent to the station
= A
? The theory of constraints identifies the constraint, exploits it fully, subordinates other steps to it and only then elevates it, for example by adding capacity.

@@ BE7 | Agile
Warehouse managers doubt that the new returns process will work. What would most help its adoption?
A) Piloting it in one warehouse, sharing the results and involving the managers in refining it
B) Introducing it in all warehouses at the same time so that no one can avoid the new process
C) Asking the managers to sign a commitment to the new process before it is introduced
D) Delaying the process until every warehouse manager agrees that it is the right approach
= A
? A pilot provides evidence, and involving sceptics in refining the process builds ownership. Forcing adoption or waiting for unanimous agreement rarely works.

@@ BE7 | Agile
The organisation's culture punishes failure, which makes people avoid the experiments that the new product approach needs. What should the project manager do?
A) Work with leaders to model and reward learning from experiments
B) Run the experiments secretly so that failures are not noticed
C) Stop experimenting and return to a fully predictive approach
D) Tell team members that failures will not be reported to anyone
= A
? Cultural change requires visible support from leaders. When leaders treat experiments as learning, people become willing to try new approaches.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
Users were trained three months before go-live and have forgotten much of what they learned. What should the project do?
A) Provide refresher sessions and job aids just before go-live, plus floor support afterwards
B) Postpone go-live by another three months so that all users can repeat the full training
C) Go live as planned, since users will quickly learn the system once they are using it every day
D) Send each user a recording of the original training to watch in their own time
= A
? Training is most effective just before people need it. Refreshers, job aids and on-the-floor support help users through the transition.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
After the organisation moves to a matrix structure, staff are confused about whom they report to and who makes which decisions. What should be done?
A) Clarify and communicate roles, responsibilities and decision rights
B) Return to the old structure until everyone is comfortable with change
C) Let staff decide for themselves whom they want to report to
D) Wait for the confusion to resolve itself as people get used to it
= A
? Structural change requires clear roles and decision rights, communicated consistently, so that people can work effectively in the new structure.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
A new global process will be introduced in offices in many countries, with different cultures and local regulations. How should the rollout be managed?
A) Adapt it to local cultures and rules, working with change agents in each office
B) Introduce it identically everywhere, since a global process must be the same in every office
C) Let each office decide whether it wants to adopt the new global process at all
D) Introduce it first at headquarters and expect the other offices to copy it later
= A
? Organisational change is more successful when it respects local culture and regulations and uses local change agents, while keeping the core process consistent.

@@ BE8 | Agile
A new AI service now offers, off the shelf, a capability that the team had planned to build itself over three months. What should the product owner do?
A) Re-evaluate the item, considering the new service if it delivers value faster or cheaper
B) Continue building the capability, since the backlog item was approved during release planning
C) Adopt the new service immediately, without any evaluation, since AI services are always better
D) Ignore the new service, since external products could change their prices in the future
= A
? Changes in the technology market can change the best way to deliver value. The product owner reassesses options, considering cost, fit, risk and time.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
New travel restrictions prevent the vendor's engineers from coming to the site to commission the equipment. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact and options, such as remote support, local partners or resequencing work
B) Wait until the travel restrictions are lifted before taking any further action
C) Ask the site team to commission the equipment without guidance from the vendor
D) Terminate the vendor's contract and look for a supplier based in the same country as the site
= A
? External changes are analysed for impact and options. Commissioning without the required expertise would create safety and quality risks.

@@ BE8 | Agile
Market data shows that younger customers increasingly abandon their carts when "buy now, pay later" is not offered. What should the product owner do?
A) Evaluate the opportunity and prioritise an experiment based on its expected value
B) Add the payment option to the next sprint, whatever is already planned for it
C) Ignore the data, since younger customers make up a small share of current sales
D) Wait for the next annual strategy review before considering the new payment option
= A
? External market trends are evaluated for their value. An experiment tests the opportunity before a large investment is made.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
The country where a key supplier is based becomes subject to international sanctions. What should the project manager do first?
A) Check the legal implications with compliance and identify alternative suppliers
B) Continue buying from the supplier, since the contract was signed before the sanctions
C) Pay the supplier through a third country so that the deliveries can continue
D) Wait to see whether the sanctions are lifted before taking any action
= A
? Sanctions are legal requirements. The project checks its obligations with compliance experts and plans alternatives; evading sanctions is illegal.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
New climate data shows that the flood risk at the construction site has increased significantly since the design was approved. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess it with experts, update the risk analysis and propose design changes through change control
B) Continue with the approved design, since it met all of the standards in force when it was approved
C) Raise the building's foundations immediately, without waiting for any approval of the change
D) Ask the insurance company to decide whether the design needs to change because of the data
= A
? Changes in the external environment are assessed with experts. Necessary design changes are proposed and approved through change control.
